    <!-- Privacy -->
    <string name="settings_privacy">Privacy</string>
    <string name="settings_privacy_open">Privacy policy</string>
    <string name="settings_privacy_summary">Llegeix collects nothing.</string>
    <string name="privacy_title">Privacy policy</string>
    <string name="privacy_body">Llegeix collects no data about you. None at all.\n\nThere is no analytics, no crash reporting, no advertising and no account. Nothing you do in this app is sent anywhere, and there is no server belonging to it to send anything to.\n\nEverything the app remembers — your collections, tags, bookmarks, saved words, reading positions and settings — is stored only on this device, in the app\'s own private storage. Uninstalling the app deletes all of it, and none of it is ever copied to a cloud backup. If you move to a new phone using Android\'s direct transfer, it comes with you: from the old phone to the new one, without passing through anybody\'s server.\n\nYour PDFs are read from wherever they already are on your device. They are never copied, uploaded or opened by anything but this app.\n\nThe app uses the internet for two things, and neither of them is about you.\n\nThe first is downloading Google\'s ML Kit translation models, once per language, so that tapping a word can translate it. That request goes to Google and is subject to Google\'s own privacy policy; the app sends no information about you with it, and no text from your documents ever leaves the device. After the models are downloaded, translation and pronunciation work entirely offline.\n\nThe second is checking for a new version of the app: at most once a day, when you open the library, and whenever you press the button in Configuració. It is what puts a red dot on the settings gear when there is a new one; there is no background service and no notification. It asks GitHub what the newest release is and, if you choose to install it, downloads it from there. The request carries no account, no identifier and nothing about your documents — it is the same anonymous request anybody can make of a public page — though GitHub, like any website, can see the address you connect from.\n\nThe app asks for permission to read files so it can find your PDFs, for network access for those two things, and for permission to hand an update to Android\'s installer, which is what actually installs it and asks you first.</string>
    <string name="privacy_updated">Last updated: 4 September 2026</string>

